    {
      "heading": "About the pronunciations",
      "text": [
        "Pronunciations follow the Hanoi (Northern) standard, written as exact (bracketed) IPA. Vietnamese is written in the Latin alphabet (chữ Quốc ngữ), one syllable at a time: a word like gia đình \"family\" is two written syllables.",
        "Every syllable has a tone, marked on its main vowel, and the tone changes the meaning: ma \"ghost\", má \"cheek\", mà \"but\". In the pronunciations the tone is shown after each syllable with pitch bars, from ˥ (high) to ˩ (low); ˀ is a catch in the throat and ʔ a glottal stop.",
        "Vietnamese has no strong word stress, but in words of more than one syllable the last syllable is a little stronger, so it carries the stress mark (ˈ).",
        "Many words differ in the South (Saigon): r, d and gi are said like English y or r instead of z, the hỏi and ngã tones merge, and some everyday words change (heo for lợn \"pig\"). The notes on those words give the Southern form."
      ]
    },
